In this lesson we'll look at how to sketch cubic and quartic polynomials when they're already in factored form. The trick is to combine four ideas. First, think about the end behaviour, which comes from the leading term. For large x, that term takes over. A positive leading cubic drops down on the left and rises up on the right, while a negative one does the opposite. A positive quartic lifts up at both ends, like a wide parabola, and a negative one falls at both ends. Next, find the intercepts. The y-intercept is just the value of the polynomial when x equals zero, and the x-intercepts are the roots. Now here's the subtle part. At each root, check how many times the factor appears. A single factor means the graph crosses straight through the axis. A squared factor means it just touches the axis, making a turning point right there on the axis. A cubed factor flattens out and crosses, like a little pause as it goes through. Finally, draw a smooth curve that respects all of that, remembering that a cubic has at most two turning points and a quartic at most three. Now let's see it in action.